INT. DIALYSIS UNIT - EVENING

Rows of recliners, each with a machine beside it, each machine with its slow red heartbeat of tubing. Late sun through blinds.

ABEL STONEHOUSE (70s, retired carpenter, flat cap in his lap) sits tethered to a machine, a crossword in his free hand. Beside him, NURSE GWENDOLYN "GWEN" TRAN (40s, scrubs patterned with cartoon owls) adjusts a line. Across the room, DR. SAMIR HADDAD-VOLKOV (50s, tie loosened, tablet clutched to his chest) reads a chart, then reads it again.

                    GWEN
          Five letters, "to compress."

                    ABEL
          Squeeze.

                    GWEN
          That's six.

                    ABEL
          Press.

                    GWEN
          There it is.

                    ABEL
          I have been doing this since before you were born, Gwen.

                    GWEN
          I was born in 1983.

                    ABEL
          Then since you were in diapers.

She laughs. He fills in the squares. Down the room, Samir hasn't moved. His thumb slides up and down the edge of the tablet.

                    GWEN
          You're ahead of schedule today.

                    ABEL
          I'm always ahead of schedule.

                    GWEN
          You've been here three hours and not once complained about the chair.

                    ABEL
          The chair's fine.

                    GWEN
          That's what concerns me.

Samir crosses to them. His smile is the kind worn for photographs.

                    SAMIR
          Abel. How are the numbers feeling?

                    ABEL
          I don't feel numbers, Doctor. I feel Tuesdays.

                    SAMIR
          Fair. Gwen, a word?

                    GWEN
          Of course.

She follows him to the hallway. Abel pretends to resume his crossword but his eyes track them. Samir speaks low. Gwen's hand goes to her mouth. She says something; Samir shakes his head. She says it again.

Abel puts down his pen.

Gwen returns first, composed, face a mask of cheer.

                    ABEL
          That was a long word.

                    GWEN
          It was a short word. The doctor just says it slowly.

                    ABEL
          Gwen.

                    GWEN
          Yes, Abel?

                    ABEL
          Seven letters. "To spare someone the truth."

                    GWEN
          (after a beat)
          I'm not good at crosswords.

Samir comes up behind her, sets the tablet in Abel's lap, turns the screen. A graph, a line, a downward slope. Abel looks at it as he might at a bad joist.

                    ABEL
          That's not a Tuesday.

                    SAMIR
          No.

                    ABEL
          How long?

                    SAMIR
          Abel --

                    ABEL
          I'm a carpenter, Samir. Measure twice.

                    SAMIR
          Months. Perhaps less.

Abel nods once. Picks up the pen. Fills one more square, slowly, in capital letters.

                    GWEN
          What did you write?

                    ABEL
          Seven letters. Protect.

He folds the crossword, tucks it into his cap, and lowers the cap onto his head.
